## 1) Severity: LOW | Type: Bug
Title: Map `order` values for the legacy box model instead of passing them through

Affected:
- app/assets/stylesheets/common/foundation/mixins.scss:125-131

Explanation:
`order` defaults to `0`, but the legacy `-webkit-box-ordinal-group` / `-moz-box-ordinal-group` properties are 1-based and default to `1`. Ordinal groups must be positive integers.

The mixin passes `$val` through unchanged, so the two models disagree:
- With the current values (2 and 3), unordered siblings get `order: 0` in the modern model but ordinal-group `1` in the legacy model. The ordering happens to come out the same.
- `@include order(0)` emits an invalid ordinal-group, so the declaration is dropped.
- A negative value, which `order` allows, is also invalid there.

Any other mix of values can reorder differently in legacy WebKit/Gecko than in modern browsers. The legacy value should be `$val + 1`.

Proposed fix:
~~~scss
@mixin order($val) {
  -webkit-box-ordinal-group: $val + 1;
  -moz-box-ordinal-group: $val + 1;
  -ms-flex-order: $val;
  -webkit-order: $val;
  order: $val;
}
~~~

## 2) Severity: LOW | Type: Bug
Title: Translate `align-items` values for legacy syntaxes and drop the non-existent `-ms-align-items`

Affected:
- app/assets/stylesheets/common/foundation/mixins.scss:117-123

Explanation:
The mixin forwards `$alignment` unchanged to every syntax, but they use different keywords:
- `-webkit-box-align` (2009) and `-ms-flex-align` (2012) use `start` / `end`.
- Modern `align-items` uses `flex-start` / `flex-end`.

Calling `@include align-items(flex-start)` therefore emits invalid legacy declarations. The current callers use `center` and `baseline`, so this is latent today, but the mixin is a shared foundation helper and will be reused.

`-ms-align-items` is not a real property; IE10 uses `-ms-flex-align`, and IE11 uses unprefixed `align-items`. It is dead output.

Also, `-moz-box` is emitted in `flexbox()`, but no `-moz-box-align` is emitted here.

Proposed fix:
~~~scss
@mixin align-items($alignment) {
  $legacy: $alignment;
  @if $alignment == flex-start { $legacy: start; }
  @else if $alignment == flex-end { $legacy: end; }
  -webkit-box-align: $legacy;
  -moz-box-align: $legacy;
  -ms-flex-align: $legacy;
  -webkit-align-items: $alignment;
  align-items: $alignment;
}
~~~

## 3) Severity: LOW | Type: Bug
Title: Long header content may overflow and push `.panel` off-screen in the new flex row

Affected:
- app/assets/stylesheets/common/base/header.scss:14-19
- app/assets/stylesheets/common/base/header.scss:35-40
- app/assets/stylesheets/common/base/topic.scss:29-37

Explanation:
`.contents` is now a flex container, and `.extra-info-wrapper` (the topic title and category shown in the header) is a flex item. Flex items default to `min-width: auto`, so they will not shrink below their content width.

Any `overflow: hidden` or `text-overflow: ellipsis` truncation on the title will stop working. A long topic title would then widen the item and push `.panel` (which relies on `margin-left: auto`) out of the header or wrap it.

With the previous float layout, the floated panel kept its space. This depends on rules for `.extra-info-wrapper` and `.title` that are outside the diff, so please verify with a very long topic title.

Separately, auto margins are not honored by the legacy `display: -webkit-box` / `-moz-box` model. On those engines `.panel` would no longer be right-aligned now that `float: right` was removed.

Proposed fix:
~~~scss
.extra-info-wrapper {
  @include order(2);
  min-width: 0;        // allow shrinking so truncation works
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  line-height: 1.5;
}
~~~
